--TEST--
`$o::VERSION` is `E0496` like `$o::f()`, with a help that names the written form: a constant is inlined where it is read, so no value — not even a `class<T>` — stands on its left — `rule:types/class-reference-sites`
--FILE--
<?nvs
class App {
    public const string VERSION = "1.2";
}
App $o = new App();
class<App> $cls = App::class as class<App>;
// An instance on the class side: the same mistake `$o::f()` makes.
echo $o::VERSION, "\n";
// A class reference is accepted by `new`, a call and `is`, and by nothing else.
echo $cls::VERSION, "\n";
// The written form, for the control.
echo App::VERSION, "\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0496]: the class side of a `::` constant must be a written class name
  --> %s:8:6
  |
8 | echo $o::VERSION, "\n";
  |      ^^ not a class name
  = help: write the class name, such as `App::VERSION`, or `self::VERSION` inside the class. A `class<T>` value works with `new`, a `::` call and `is`, but not with a constant

error[E0496]: the class side of a `::` constant must be a written class name
   --> %s:10:6
   |
10 | echo $cls::VERSION, "\n";
   |      ^^^^ not a class name
   = help: write the class name, such as `App::VERSION`, or `self::VERSION` inside the class. A `class<T>` value works with `new`, a `::` call and `is`, but not with a constant

error: aborting due to 2 errors
